# Request retry helper

## withBackoff

```typescript
const RETRYABLE = new Set([429, 502, 503, 504]);

export async function withBackoff<T>(
  fn: () => Promise<T>,
  opts: { attempts?: number; baseMs?: number; capMs?: number } = {},
): Promise<T> {
  const attempts = opts.attempts ?? 5;
  const base = opts.baseMs ?? 200;
  const cap = opts.capMs ?? 10_000;
  let lastError: unknown;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
      const status = (err as { status?: number }).status;
      if (status !== undefined && !RETRYABLE.has(status)) throw err;
      const jitter = Math.random() * base;
      await sleep(Math.min(cap, base * 2 ** i + jitter));
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```

Each attempt waits twice as long as the previous one, plus jitter, so a
struggling upstream is not hit by every client at the same instant. Only 429 and
5xx statuses are retried; a 400 is a bug in the caller and is rethrown at once.

# Payment webhook endpoint

## Signature check and idempotency

```typescript
export async function handleStripeWebhook(req: Request): Promise<Response> {
  const signature = req.headers.get("stripe-signature");
  const raw = await req.text();
  let event: Stripe.Event;
  try {
    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(raw, signature!, WEBHOOK_SECRET);
  } catch {
    return new Response("bad signature", { status: 400 });
  }

  const seen = await db.get("SELECT 1 FROM processed_events WHERE id = ?", event.id);
  if (seen) return new Response("duplicate", { status: 200 });

  switch (event.type) {
    case "checkout.session.completed":
      await fulfillOrder(event.data.object as Stripe.Checkout.Session);
      break;
    case "invoice.payment_failed":
      await flagDelinquent(event.data.object as Stripe.Invoice);
      break;
  }
  await db.run("INSERT INTO processed_events (id, at) VALUES (?, ?)", event.id, Date.now());
  return new Response("ok");
}
```

Stripe delivers the same event more than once whenever an endpoint is slow to
answer, so every event id is recorded and a repeat delivery is answered without
running the side effect a second time.

# Direct-to-storage uploads

## Presigned PUT

```typescript
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";
import { getSignedUrl } from "@aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner";

const s3 = new S3Client({ region: "eu-central-1" });

export async function uploadTicket(userId: string, contentType: string) {
  const key = `uploads/${userId}/${crypto.randomUUID()}`;
  const url = await getSignedUrl(
    s3,
    new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: "user-media", Key: key, ContentType: contentType }),
    { expiresIn: 900 },
  );
  return { url, key };
}
```

The browser sends the bytes to object storage itself; the API never holds the
file in memory and never proxies it. The signed URL expires after 15 minutes and
is bound to the exact content type the client declared.

# GraphQL user resolvers

## Batched lookups

```typescript
const userLoader = new DataLoader<string, User>(async (ids) => {
  const rows = await db.all("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id IN (" + ids.map(() => "?").join(",") + ")", ...ids);
  const byId = new Map(rows.map((r) => [r.id, r]));
  return ids.map((id) => byId.get(id) ?? new Error("not found"));
});

export const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    user: (_: unknown, { id }: { id: string }) => userLoader.load(id),
  },
  Order: {
    customer: (order: Order) => userLoader.load(order.userId),
  },
};
```

Without the loader a list of 50 orders issues 51 queries. DataLoader collects
every load call made during one tick and turns them into a single IN query.

